\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Subdiffusive random growth of cell volume. (a) $\sigma ^{2}_x (\Delta t)$ vs. the relative age for time intervals $\Delta t = 1, 2, 3,$ and $4$ min for the simulation data of the null model. (b) $\sigma ^{2}_x (\Delta t)$ vs. $\Delta t$ for several relative ages, from which we calculated the diffusion exponent. Curves for different relative ages are vertically shifted for visual clarity to highlight the robust power-law scaling. (c) The diffusion exponent ($\alpha $) calculated from the simulation data as a function of the relative age, showing the reliable regime where $\alpha $ equals the ground truth $1$. (d-f) The same analysis as (a-c) but for the same experimental data of {\it  E. coli} in Fig.~\ref {fig:traj}(a). (g-i) The same as (a-c) but for the viscoelastic model. The dashed line in (c, f, i) is the average exponent in the reliable regime.}}{3}{}}
\newlabel{fig:fluctuation}{{2}{3}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces The viscoelastic model of the cell wall reproduces the subdiffusive random growth. (a) We model the cell wall as a serial connection of multiple Voigt elements, each with an elastic component (the spring) and a viscous component (the dashpot). We assume that the relaxation time of each element is random due to the random elastic constant, such that the relaxation time follows a power-law distribution. (b) The same as Fig.~\ref {fig:traj}(a), but we replace the white noise with the colored noise $\xi (t)$ generated by the random Voigt model. (c-d) Distributions of the normalized doubling time $T/\overline  {T}$ (c) and normalized added volume throughout one cell cycle $\Delta V/\overline  {\Delta V}$ (d) generated by the viscoelastic model simulation. In (b-d) and Fig.~\ref {fig:fluctuation}(g-i), we set the dimensionless noise strength $A_{\sigma } \approx 3.2 \times 10^{-2}$ so that the simulated $\sigma _x^2$ at $\Delta t=1$ min is approximately the same as in the experiments. $\tau _0 = 0.001$,}}{4}{}}
